# AWS CCP — Security & Compliance

In the shared responsibility model, AWS is responsible for security {{of}} the cloud. Customers are responsible for security in the cloud.
---
AWS runs the facilities, hardware, and hypervisor. You secure what you build on top.

Who is responsible for patching the guest operating system on an EC2 instance?
---
The customer
~ AWS
~ Both equally
~ The AWS Marketplace vendor
---
AWS patches the hypervisor and hardware. Everything inside your instance is yours.

Who is responsible for the physical security of AWS data centers?
---
AWS
~ The customer
~ Both equally
~ A third-party auditor
---
Customers never see the inside of a data center. Compliance reports in AWS Artifact cover it.

Who is responsible for enabling encryption on data stored in S3?
---
The customer
~ AWS
~ Both equally
~ No one, S3 rejects unencrypted data
---
AWS provides the tools (SSE, KMS) but configuring protection of customer data is on you.

Which service controls who can do what in your AWS account with users, groups, roles, and policies?
---
IAM
= AWS IAM
= Identity and Access Management
---
Global service, no charge. The foundation every other service's permissions rest on.

Which IAM identity should an application running on EC2 use to get credentials?
---
An IAM role
~ An IAM user with access keys
~ The root user
~ An IAM group
---
Roles hand out temporary credentials. Never bake long-lived access keys into an instance.

What is an IAM policy?
---
A JSON document that allows or denies actions on resources
~ A password rule for the account
~ A firewall rule for a VPC
~ A billing alert threshold
---
Attach policies to users, groups, or roles. An explicit deny always wins.

Granting only the permissions a task actually requires is the principle of least {{privilege}}.
---
Start from nothing and add what is needed, not the other way around.

What is the best practice for the account root user?
---
Enable MFA and use it only for tasks that require it
~ Use it for daily administration
~ Share its credentials with all admins
~ Delete it after creating an admin user
---
Almost everything should be done by IAM identities. The root user cannot be deleted.

What does MFA stand for?
---
multi-factor authentication
= multifactor authentication
= multi factor authentication
---
Something you know plus something you have. Turn it on for root first.

Which service provides single sign-on access to multiple AWS accounts and business applications?
---
IAM Identity Center
= AWS IAM Identity Center
= Identity Center
= AWS SSO
---
The successor to AWS Single Sign-On.

Which service continuously monitors your account for threats and malicious activity using machine learning?
---
GuardDuty
= Amazon GuardDuty
---
It analyzes CloudTrail events, VPC Flow Logs, and DNS logs. Detection only, it does not block.

Which service scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities?
---
Inspector
= Amazon Inspector
---
Think CVEs and unintended network exposure. GuardDuty watches behavior, Inspector checks software.

Which service uses machine learning to discover sensitive data such as PII in S3 buckets?
---
Macie
= Amazon Macie
---
Personally identifiable information: names, card numbers, credentials.

Which service protects applications against DDoS attacks?
---
Shield
= AWS Shield
---
Shield Standard is free and always on. Shield Advanced adds cost protection and a response team.

Which service filters malicious web requests such as SQL injection and cross-site scripting?
---
WAF
= AWS WAF
= web application firewall
---
Sits in front of CloudFront, ALB, or API Gateway with allow, block, and rate-based rules.

Which service creates and manages the encryption keys used across AWS?
---
KMS
= AWS KMS
= Key Management Service
---
Most services encrypt at rest with a checkbox because KMS is behind it.

Which service provides dedicated, single-tenant hardware modules for storing encryption keys?
---
CloudHSM
~ KMS
~ Secrets Manager
~ Certificate Manager
---
KMS is managed and multi-tenant. CloudHSM gives you your own device for strict compliance regimes.

Which service stores database credentials and API keys and rotates them automatically?
---
Secrets Manager
= AWS Secrets Manager
---
Rotation without redeploying code. Parameter Store is the free cousin without built-in rotation.

Which service aggregates findings from GuardDuty, Inspector, and Macie into a single dashboard?
---
Security Hub
= AWS Security Hub
---
One score, one feed, checks against standards like CIS.

Which service gives on-demand access to AWS compliance reports such as SOC and PCI?
---
Artifact
= AWS Artifact
---
Self-service downloads for your auditors. No support ticket needed.

A security group is {{stateful}}, so return traffic for an allowed connection is automatically allowed.
---
Network ACLs are stateless and evaluate rules in both directions.

At which level does a security group operate?
---
The instance level
~ The subnet level
~ The VPC level
~ The Region level
---
Network ACLs operate at the subnet level. Security groups only have allow rules, NACLs have both.
